cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  eps-ground-rupture — interactive companion to Chiama et al. (2025)
message: >-
  This record identifies the software and the companion website. If you use the
  analyses or the data behind them, cite the paper under `preferred-citation`
  as well — and, for the data itself, the archives it comes from (DesignSafe-CI
  for the DEM experiments; the field compilations carry their own citations).
type: software
version: 0.4.0
date-released: 2026-10-13
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: https://github.com/HarvardRC/eps-ground-rupture
url: https://harvardrc.github.io/eps-ground-rupture/
abstract: >-
  A reproducible data-processing pipeline and companion website for the two-
  dimensional distinct element method (DEM) surface-rupture analyses of Chiama
  et al. (2025). The website brings together five interactive Tableau Public
  dashboards and the Ground Rupture Explorer. The pipeline cleans and
  organizes published simulation outputs and field measurements into Parquet
  tables, defines analytical views in DuckDB, checks results with automated
  tests, and exports the data used by the dashboards and explorer. The site
  explains the analyses and documents differences from the paper's published
  figures.

  The Ground Rupture Explorer lets readers choose modeled geological
  conditions and a range of fault slip (movement), then examine the
  corresponding results from existing simulations. It shows distributions and
  summary statistics for scarp height, deformation zone width, and scarp dip.
  Readers can download a ZIP containing the matching simulation records and
  summary statistics as CSV files, the scenario settings, a record of data
  sources and versions, and a Microsoft Word report containing the scenario,
  statistics, and charts. Each chart can also be saved as an image. This
  research tool helps readers investigate simulated ground rupture. It does
  not predict what will happen at a particular site.
keywords:
  - earthquake
  - surface rupture
  - distinct element method
  - fault displacement hazard
  - scarp morphology
  - reproducible research
